      "textEn": "He resides in the womb of the pregnant woman. The yearly and male-only vow is in vain. If you hold on to them, you will be ruined, son. 54. Meaning of the word.\n\nSamvatsaram—Up to one year; Punsavanam— named after Punsavana; Vratam— vow; Etat— this; Aviplutam— without breaking, without transgression; Dharayishyasi— you will do; Chet— if; Tubhyam— yours; Shakra-ha— slayer of Indra; Bhavita— will be; Suta— son. Kashyap Muni further said—If you, with faith, for at least one year,\nremaining engaged in this vow, perform this Punsavana ritual, then you will have a son who will\nkill Indra. But if any error remains in doing this vow, then your son will become Indra's friend.\nWill become. Having approached the flood, O king, the great mind.\nHaving taken the vow of Kashyapa's womb, he held the moon. 55 Meaning of the word\n\nBadham—so be it; Iti—in this way; Abhyupeetya—accepting; Ath—then; Ditiha—by Diti; Rajan—Oh King; Maha-\nManah—Delighted; Kashyapat—From Kashyap; Garbham—Virya; Adhatta—Having obtained; Vratam—Vow; Cha—And; Anjah—Properly;\nDadhar—Followed; Sa—He. Oh, King Parikshit! Kashyap's wife, Diti, adopted the purification ritual called Punsavan. He said, \"Yes, I will follow your instructions.\" She\nWith great joy, assuming Kashyap's semen, became pregnant and continued to observe the vow with faith. Matrushvasurbhirbhayamindra ajnaya mana. The poet wandered in the hermitage of the patient. 56. Lexicon"
